"""Guards the SITE's wiring of a display face built with the font-design skill: the stylesheet, the
files it names, the leftovers of the face it replaced, the preload, and the CSS rules the axes rely on.

WHAT IT'S FOR: this is a TEMPLATE. Fill every double-brace slot below and copy the file into the site repo's
test directory; the font file itself is covered by scripts/tests/test_font.py, this file covers the
page around it. Each guard covers one way the wiring could break while every page still looks
plausible, because a fallback face sits behind the custom one in every stack: a stylesheet pointing at
a file that is not there or not tracked; a stack that still names the old face; a title using a
character the font lacks, which then renders that one character in the fallback; a fluid size that
leaves the opsz axis; a small title that lost its Medium; a letter-spacing that doubles the display
cut's own track; a page without the preload, so the first paint is the fallback.

Slots, each written in the code below as the name between double braces (all strings unless noted),
with the Bract values as the worked example:
  family             the CSS family name, e.g. Bract
  static_root        repo-relative directory the site serves as its web root, e.g. static-pages
  css_path           repo-relative stylesheet, e.g. static-pages/css/site.css
  templates_glob     repo-relative glob of the page templates, e.g. templates/*.html
  built_pages_glob   repo-relative glob of the built pages, e.g. static-pages/**/*.html
  font_url           the root-relative URL of the variable woff2, e.g. /fonts/bract/Bract-VF.woff2
  italic_font_url    the italic group's woff2 URL, or empty when no italic group is built
  preload_tag        the exact preload tag every template carries (crossorigin included)
  stylesheet_tag     the exact stylesheet link tag the preload must precede
  static_config      repo-relative static-serving config (app.yaml, nginx conf, _headers), e.g. app.yaml;
                     empty when the host serves every file type without an allowlist, which skips (f) with
                     the reason printed. NOT the stylesheet: it names woff2 in its url(), so (f) would pass
                     for nothing, and the test refuses it by name (second dry run, finding 9)
  old_family_pattern regex for the replaced face's names, e.g. Bodoni|Didot; empty when the old face
                     stays in use elsewhere (a system font also used for body text), which skips (c)
  old_files_glob     glob under static_root for the replaced face's files, e.g. fonts/bodoni-*.woff2;
                     empty when there are none to remove, which skips (d)
  display_selectors  Python list of the selectors set in the DISPLAY cut (at or above the opsz start, whose
                     advances carry the display concept's track), e.g. [".prose h1", ".frontispiece-title"]. A
                     selector set in the face below the opsz start is in the text cut, whose track is 0, so a
                     tracked caps wordmark at 22 px keeps its letter-spacing and goes in text_cut_selectors
                     instead; guard (g) still covers it, because (g) reads the stylesheet
  text_cut_selectors Python list of the selectors set in the face in the TEXT cut (below the opsz start), e.g.
                     [".wordmark"], or [] when there are none. Guard (s) requires every selector whose stylesheet
                     rule sets the family to be in one of the two lists, so a display rule added later cannot
                     stay out of guards (p) and (r) unnoticed (independent re-review, code-b F008)
  medium_selectors   Python list of the small-title selectors that must be weight 500, e.g. [".plate-title"]
  opsz_max_px        number: the largest display size in CSS px, which must equal the opsz axis maximum, e.g. 80
  fallback_family    OPTIONAL guard (t). The family name of the fallback @font-face with metric overrides, e.g. "Bract Fallback":
                     a `local()` face with size-adjust and the three metric overrides, listed right after the display
                     family in every display stack, so the first paint in the fallback takes the same room as the
                     font. Empty skips (t) with the reason printed. `scripts/fallback-metrics.py` prints the values
  balance_selectors  OPTIONAL guard (u). Python list of the display selectors that must carry `text-wrap: balance`
                     (no widow on a wrapped headline without a hand-placed no-break space), e.g. [".prose h1"];
                     [] skips (u) with the reason printed. LIMIT: the cascade is not computed; it reads only the `text-wrap`
                     declared on these selectors, so `.hero { text-wrap: wrap }` on `<h1 class="hero">` is not seen
  font_synthesis     "adopted" when the site declares font-synthesis: none on its display rules and (r) must
                     hold, "declined" when the operator declined it, which skips (r) with the reason printed;
                     any other value fails (r) naming the two words, so the decision is recorded in the slot

How the stylesheet is read (dry-run finding W12: the first template read one quote style, one selector
per rule and clamp() sizes only, and refused a stylesheet written any other way): comments are
stripped, then every `selector-list { declarations }` block is collected, the blocks nested in an
at-rule (@media, @supports) included; a rule whose selector list names several selectors
(`h3, .plate-title { ... }`) counts for each of them; a family name is read with single quotes, double
quotes or none; a font-size is a clamp() (its third argument), a rem, a px or an em value. Exact
guarantees: declarations are read as `property: value` pairs, so the `font` shorthand is not expanded
and a display rule must set `font-family` and `font-size` as longhands; an em size is taken at the
16 px root; a vw-only size is refused by name; display_selectors() reads tags, classes, tag.class
compounds and descendant chains of those (a child combinator `>` is read as a descendant: over-inclusive,
which can only make the coverage test stricter) and raises on anything else rather than guessing. The
static-config test is a presence check for the font's extension, not a proof of routing: a site with
its own reachability test should keep that one instead.

Every test was watched failing under its named mutation when the template was derived (Bract, 2026-11-15)
and again when it was rewritten for W12 against the skill's fixture site (tests/test_site_wiring_template.py
in the skill repo); re-run the mutations in the site after filling the slots (see references/testing.md).

Copyright 2026 John Knox. MIT licence.
"""
import pathlib
import re
import shutil
import subprocess
from html.parser import HTMLParser

import pytest
from fontTools.ttLib import TTFont

REPO = pathlib.Path(__file__).resolve().parents[1]  # this file lives one directory below the site root (tests/)
FAMILY = "{{ family }}"
STATIC = REPO / "{{ static_root }}"
CSS = REPO / "{{ css_path }}"
TEMPLATES = sorted(REPO.glob("{{ templates_glob }}"))
BUILT_PAGES_GLOB = "{{ built_pages_glob }}"
FONT_URL = "{{ font_url }}"
ITALIC_FONT_URL = "{{ italic_font_url }}"
WOFF2 = STATIC / FONT_URL.lstrip("/")
PRELOAD = '{{ preload_tag }}'
STYLESHEET_TAG = '{{ stylesheet_tag }}'
STATIC_CONFIG = REPO / "{{ static_config }}"
OLD_FAMILY_PATTERN = r"{{ old_family_pattern }}"
OLD_FILES_GLOB = "{{ old_files_glob }}"
DISPLAY_SELECTORS = {{ display_selectors }}
TEXT_CUT_SELECTORS = {{ text_cut_selectors }}
MEDIUM_SELECTORS = {{ medium_selectors }}
OPSZ_MAX_PX = {{ opsz_max_px }}
FONT_SYNTHESIS = "{{ font_synthesis }}"
FALLBACK_FAMILY = "{{ fallback_family }}"
BALANCE_SELECTORS = {{ balance_selectors }}
URL_IN_CSS = re.compile(r"""url\(\s*['"]?([^'")]+?)['"]?\s*\)""")
FONT_EXT = re.compile(r"\.(?:woff2|woff|ttf|otf)$")


def bare(url):
    """A url() without its query string or fragment: `/fonts/x.woff2?v=3` names the file `/fonts/x.woff2`."""
    return re.split(r"[?#]", url, maxsplit=1)[0]


def css():
    return CSS.read_text()


def css_blocks():
    """[(selector list, declarations)] for every rule of the stylesheet in source order, with comments
    stripped. A block nested inside an at-rule that holds rules (@media, @supports) is listed as its own
    rule; an at-rule that holds declarations (@font-face) is listed under its own name."""
    text = re.sub(r"/\*.*?\*/", "", css(), flags=re.S)
    out = []

    def walk(s):
        i = 0
        while True:
            j = s.find("{", i)
            if j < 0:
                return
            head = s[i:j].rsplit(";", 1)[-1].strip()  # drop a preceding statement such as @import ...;
            depth, k = 1, j + 1
            while k < len(s) and depth:
                depth += {"{": 1, "}": -1}.get(s[k], 0)
                k += 1
            body = s[j + 1:k - 1]
            if head.startswith("@") and "{" in body:
                walk(body)
            else:
                out.append((head, body))
            i = k
    walk(text)
    return out


def norm(selector):
    return re.sub(r"\s+", " ", selector.strip())


def selectors_of(head):
    return [norm(s) for s in head.split(",") if s.strip()]


def declarations(body):
    """{property: value} of one block, the last value winning when a property repeats."""
    out = {}
    for decl in body.split(";"):
        prop, sep, value = decl.partition(":")
        if sep:
            out[prop.strip().lower()] = value.strip()
    return out


def first_family(value):
    """The first family of a font-family value, without its quotes."""
    return value.split(",")[0].strip().strip("'\"")


def rules_for(selector):
    """The declaration blocks whose selector list contains `selector` (whitespace-normalised), in source
    order; a rule written for a list of selectors counts for each of them."""
    want = norm(selector)
    found = [body for head, body in css_blocks() if want in selectors_of(head)]
    assert found, f"no rule for {selector!r} in {CSS.name} (a selector list such as `h3, {selector}` counts; a selector spelled differently does not)"
    return found


def declared(selector, prop):
    """Every value of `prop` declared for `selector`, across all its blocks, in source order."""
    return [d[prop] for d in (declarations(b) for b in rules_for(selector)) if prop in d]


def size_max_px(value, selector):
    """The largest CSS px a font-size value can reach: clamp()'s third argument, else the value itself;
    rem and em at the 16 px root, px as written. Anything else (vw alone, calc(), a keyword) is refused."""
    m = re.fullmatch(r"clamp\(\s*[^,]+,\s*[^,]+,\s*([^)]+)\)", value.strip(), re.I)
    v = (m.group(1) if m else value).strip()
    m = re.fullmatch(r"([\d.]+)(rem|em|px)", v, re.I)
    assert m, f"{selector}: cannot read the largest size from font-size: {value!r} (a clamp() max, rem, em or px is expected)"
    return float(m.group(1)) * (16 if m.group(2).lower() in ("rem", "em") else 1)


def built_pages():
    pages = sorted(REPO.glob(BUILT_PAGES_GLOB))
    assert pages, f"no built pages match {BUILT_PAGES_GLOB}; build the site first"
    return pages


@pytest.fixture(scope="module")
def font():
    assert WOFF2.is_file(), f"{WOFF2} does not exist"
    return TTFont(WOFF2)


# --- a-f: the stylesheet, the files, the leftovers ---------------------------------------------------
def test_the_stylesheet_declares_one_variable_face_per_style(font):
    """(a) One @font-face per style carries every weight. It must declare the weight RANGE the fvar axis
    spans, or the weights between fall back; the range is read from the shipped font, not typed here.
    The roman's src is FONT_URL; an italic group (ITALIC_FONT_URL set) is a second face, font-style italic."""
    faces = [declarations(body) for head, body in css_blocks() if head == "@font-face"]
    mine = [f for f in faces if first_family(f.get("font-family", "")) == FAMILY]
    by_style = {}
    for f in mine:
        by_style.setdefault(f.get("font-style", "normal").lower(), []).append(f)
    want = {"normal": FONT_URL} | ({"italic": ITALIC_FONT_URL} if ITALIC_FONT_URL else {})
    assert set(by_style) == set(want), f"@font-face styles for {FAMILY}: {sorted(by_style)}, expected {sorted(want)} (one variable file per style)"
    wght = next((a for a in font["fvar"].axes if a.axisTag == "wght"), None)
    assert wght, "the font has no wght axis"
    lo, hi = int(wght.minValue), int(wght.maxValue)
    for style, url in want.items():
        assert len(by_style[style]) == 1, f"{len(by_style[style])} @font-face rules for {FAMILY} {style}: the variable file carries every weight, so one is enough"
        f = by_style[style][0]
        assert f.get("font-weight", "").split() == [str(lo), str(hi)], f"the {style} face must declare the weight RANGE {lo} {hi}, or intermediate weights fall back (has {f.get('font-weight')!r})"
        assert f.get("font-display") == "swap", f"the {style} face must set font-display: swap"
        assert [bare(u) for u in URL_IN_CSS.findall(f.get("src", ""))] == [url], f"the {style} face's src must be url({url}), has {f.get('src')!r}"


def test_each_declared_face_file_carries_the_style_it_is_declared_for():
    """(a2) The file behind the italic @font-face must BE an italic font (post.italicAngle set, the fsSelection
    ITALIC bit, head.macStyle italic) and the roman's an upright one: (a) checks the declaration and (b) that a
    file exists, so the roman woff2 named as the italic src passed both (independent re-review, code-b F009),
    and the browser then slants the roman itself. The three bits are the ones scripts/build.py sets per group."""
    want = {"normal": FONT_URL} | ({"italic": ITALIC_FONT_URL} if ITALIC_FONT_URL else {})
    for style, url in want.items():
        path = STATIC / url.lstrip("/")
        assert path.is_file(), f"{url} does not exist"
        f = TTFont(path)
        italic = style == "italic"
        got = {"post.italicAngle": f["post"].italicAngle, "fsSelection ITALIC": bool(f["OS/2"].fsSelection & 0x01), "macStyle italic": bool(f["head"].macStyle & 0x02)}
        assert (got["post.italicAngle"] != 0) == italic and got["fsSelection ITALIC"] == italic and got["macStyle italic"] == italic, \
            f"{url} is declared as the {style} face but its tables say {got}"


def test_every_font_url_in_the_stylesheet_has_a_file():
    """(b) A url() with no file behind it is a 404 the browser hides behind the fallback face."""
    urls = sorted({bare(u) for u in URL_IN_CSS.findall(css()) if FONT_EXT.search(bare(u))})
    assert FONT_URL in urls, f"{CSS.name} does not reference {FONT_URL}"
    for url in urls:
        assert url.startswith("/"), f"{url} is not root-relative; this test resolves font URLs under {STATIC.name}"
        assert (STATIC / url.lstrip("/")).is_file(), f"{url} is referenced by {CSS.name} and does not exist"


def test_nothing_names_the_old_face_any_more():
    """(c) A stack that still names the replaced face serves it where it is installed and the
    fallback where it is not: two renderings of one page."""
    if not OLD_FAMILY_PATTERN:
        pytest.skip("old_family_pattern is empty: the replaced face stays in use elsewhere, so its name is allowed (decision recorded in the slot)")
    old = re.compile(OLD_FAMILY_PATTERN)
    offenders = [p for p in [CSS, *TEMPLATES, *built_pages()] if old.search(p.read_text())]
    assert not offenders, f"still name the old display face: {[str(p.relative_to(REPO)) for p in offenders[:5]]}"


def test_the_old_font_files_are_gone():
    """(d) Files nothing references still ship; the old face's files must be removed, not left behind."""
    if not OLD_FILES_GLOB:
        pytest.skip("old_files_glob is empty: the replaced face shipped no files of its own (decision recorded in the slot)")
    left = sorted(STATIC.glob(OLD_FILES_GLOB))
    assert not left, f"the replaced face's files are still in the web root: {[str(p.relative_to(REPO)) for p in left]}"


def test_shipped_fonts_are_tracked():
    """(e) The web root uploads wholesale and nothing regenerates a font during deploy, so an untracked
    font vanishes on a fresh clone and a tracked-but-deleted one ships a 404. The claim is about git, so
    outside a git work tree (or without git) the test SKIPS saying so, and the site records how its fonts
    are kept instead; it does not pass."""
    font_dir = (STATIC / FONT_URL.lstrip("/")).parent
    on_disk = sorted(str(p.relative_to(REPO)) for p in font_dir.rglob("*.woff2"))
    git = shutil.which("git")
    inside = git and subprocess.run([git, "rev-parse", "--is-inside-work-tree"], cwd=REPO, capture_output=True, text=True)
    if not git or inside.returncode != 0 or inside.stdout.strip() != "true":
        pytest.skip("not a git work tree (or no git on PATH): the tracked-fonts guard cannot run here; record how the fonts are kept")
    r = subprocess.run([git, "ls-files", str(font_dir.relative_to(REPO))], cwd=REPO, capture_output=True, text=True)
    assert r.returncode == 0, f"git ls-files failed in {REPO}: {r.stderr.strip()}"
    tracked = r.stdout.split()
    assert on_disk == sorted(t for t in tracked if t.endswith(".woff2")), f"woff2 on disk and in git differ under {font_dir.relative_to(REPO)}"


def test_the_static_config_serves_the_font_extension():
    """(f) A static-serving allowlist that omits woff2 works on every dev server and 404s in production.
    Presence check only: the extension must appear in the config. A site with a reachability test
    (every built file matched by a handler) should keep that one instead of this. An empty slot skips
    with the reason; the stylesheet is refused as the config, because its url() names woff2 whatever
    the host serves (second dry run, finding 9)."""
    if not "{{ static_config }}".strip():
        pytest.skip("static_config is empty: the host serves every file type without an allowlist (decision recorded in the slot)")
    assert STATIC_CONFIG.resolve() != CSS.resolve(), \
        f"static_config names the stylesheet {CSS.name}, whose url() mentions woff2 whatever the host serves; name the serving config, or leave the slot empty"
    text = STATIC_CONFIG.read_text()
    assert "woff2" in text, f"{STATIC_CONFIG.name} never mentions woff2; the font would 404 in production"


# --- g: coverage ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
COMPOUND = re.compile(r"^(h[1-6]|[a-z][a-z0-9-]*)?((?:\.[\w-]+)*)$")


def parse_chain(selector):
    """A selector as a list of (tag or None, {classes}) compounds, outermost first; raises on anything
    display_selectors() does not read. `a > b` is read as `a b`."""
    chain = []
    for part in norm(selector).replace(">", " ").split():
        m = COMPOUND.match(part)
        if not m or part == "":
            raise AssertionError(f"display selector {selector!r} is not a chain of tags, classes and tag.class compounds; teach display_selectors() to read it")
        chain.append((m.group(1), set(c for c in m.group(2).split(".") if c)))
    assert chain, f"empty display selector {selector!r}"
    return chain


def display_selectors():
    """The selector chains whose CSS rule sets the display face, read from the stylesheet so a new
    display-set class is covered without being listed here."""
    chains = []
    for head, body in css_blocks():
        if head.startswith("@"):
            continue
        d = declarations(body)
        if "font-family" not in d or first_family(d["font-family"]) != FAMILY:
            continue
        chains += [parse_chain(s) for s in selectors_of(head)]
    assert chains, f"no rule in {CSS.name} sets font-family: {FAMILY}"
    return chains


class DisplayText(HTMLParser):
    """Collects the text a page sets in the display face: every element matched by a display selector
    chain (the last compound on the element, the earlier ones on its ancestors in order), with its
    descendants. Exact guarantee: elements made display-face by any OTHER route (inline style, a
    selector display_selectors() does not read) are not seen; including too much can only make the
    test stricter."""

    def __init__(self, chains):
        super().__init__()
        self.chains, self.stack, self.chars = chains, [], {}

    @staticmethod
    def fits(compound, node):
        tag, classes = compound
        return (tag is None or tag == node[0]) and classes <= node[1]

    def matched(self, node):
        for chain in self.chains:
            if not self.fits(chain[-1], node):
                continue
            ancestors, ok = list(reversed(self.stack)), True
            for compound in reversed(chain[:-1]):
                while ancestors and not self.fits(compound, ancestors[0][:2]):
                    ancestors.pop(0)
                if not ancestors:
                    ok = False
                    break
                ancestors.pop(0)
            if ok:
                return True
        return False

    def handle_starttag(self, tag, attrs):
        if tag in ("br", "img", "hr", "meta", "link", "input"):
            return
        node = (tag, set((dict(attrs).get("class") or "").split()))
        mine = self.matched(node) or (bool(self.stack) and self.stack[-1][2])
        self.stack.append((node[0], node[1], mine))

    def handle_endtag(self, tag):
        if tag not in (t for t, _, _ in self.stack):
            return
        while self.stack:
            t, _, _ = self.stack.pop()
            if t == tag:
                break

    def handle_data(self, data):
        if self.stack and self.stack[-1][2]:
            for c in data:
                if not c.isspace() or c == " ":
                    self.chars.setdefault(c, None)


def family_selectors():
    """The selector texts (whitespace-normalised) of every stylesheet rule that sets the family first."""
    out = []
    for head, body in css_blocks():
        if head.startswith("@"):
            continue
        d = declarations(body)
        if "font-family" in d and first_family(d["font-family"]) == FAMILY:
            out += [s for s in selectors_of(head) if s not in out]
    return out


def test_every_selector_set_in_the_face_is_listed_as_display_or_text_cut():
    """(s) Guards (p) and (r) run over the display_selectors slot, a hand-filled list, so a rule that sets the
    family on a new selector was outside both until it was added by hand (independent re-review, code-b F008).
    Every selector whose rule sets the family must be in display_selectors or text_cut_selectors, and the
    display list must not be empty, or (p) runs over nothing and (r)'s adopted branch passes on nothing."""
    assert DISPLAY_SELECTORS, "display_selectors is empty: guards (p) and (r) would check no rule"
    listed = {norm(s) for s in DISPLAY_SELECTORS} | {norm(s) for s in TEXT_CUT_SELECTORS}
    both = {norm(s) for s in DISPLAY_SELECTORS} & {norm(s) for s in TEXT_CUT_SELECTORS}
    assert not both, f"in both display_selectors and text_cut_selectors: {sorted(both)}"
    unlisted = [s for s in family_selectors() if s not in listed]
    assert not unlisted, f"set in {FAMILY} by {CSS.name} but in neither display_selectors nor text_cut_selectors: {unlisted}; " \
                         f"add each to the list for its cut so guards (p) and (r) cover it"
    unknown = sorted(listed - set(family_selectors()))
    assert not unknown, f"listed as display or text-cut selectors but no rule in {CSS.name} sets {FAMILY} on them: {unknown}"


def test_the_font_covers_every_character_the_display_face_sets(font):
    """(g) A character the pages set in the display face that the font lacks renders in the fallback,
    one letter at a time; the page looks fine from across the room."""
    chains = display_selectors()
    used = {}
    for page in built_pages():
        p = DisplayText(chains)
        p.feed(page.read_text())
        for c in p.chars:
            used.setdefault(c, page.relative_to(REPO).as_posix())
    assert used, "no display-face text found in any built page"
    cmap = font.getBestCmap()
    missing = {c: used[c] for c in used if ord(c) not in cmap}
    assert not missing, f"{FAMILY} lacks {missing} (character: first page using it); each would render in the fallback face"


# --- n-r: the CSS the axes depend on ---------------------------------------------------------------------
def test_fluid_sizes_stay_inside_the_opsz_axis(font):
    """(n) Above the axis maximum the display cut is clamped, not extended; the largest title must sit
    on the axis end exactly so the review's reading at that size is what ships. The largest size each
    display selector can reach is read across all its rules, a nested @media rule included."""
    opsz = next((a for a in font["fvar"].axes if a.axisTag == "opsz"), None)
    if opsz is None:
        pytest.skip("the font has no opsz axis; decision recorded in font.json (one-axis group)")
    sizes = {s: [size_max_px(v, s) for v in declared(s, "font-size")] for s in DISPLAY_SELECTORS}
    assert any(sizes.values()), f"no display selector declares a font-size: {DISPLAY_SELECTORS}"
    biggest = max(px for v in sizes.values() for px in v)
    assert biggest == opsz.maxValue == OPSZ_MAX_PX, f"largest display size {biggest}px vs opsz max {opsz.maxValue} vs expected {OPSZ_MAX_PX}"


@pytest.mark.parametrize("selector", MEDIUM_SELECTORS)
def test_small_titles_and_numerals_are_medium(selector):
    """(o) The display elements under the Medium threshold take weight 500 so their hairlines stay above
    the floor on a 1x screen; Regular there was Bract's first readability finding. Every weight declared
    for the selector must be 500: a narrow-width @media rule that drops it back to 400 fails too."""
    weights = declared(selector, "font-weight")
    assert weights and all(w == "500" for w in weights), f"{selector} must set font-weight: 500 in every rule that sets a weight (has {weights})"


@pytest.mark.parametrize("selector", DISPLAY_SELECTORS)
def test_display_rules_carry_no_letter_spacing(selector):
    """(p) A display cut carries its own track in its advances (review finding F005), so a CSS
    letter-spacing on the same rule doubles it (trap 27)."""
    assert not declared(selector, "letter-spacing"), f"{selector} sets letter-spacing"


def test_every_template_preloads_the_font():
    """(q) font-display: swap means the first paint uses the fallback unless the font arrives first;
    the preload starts the fetch before the stylesheet is parsed. crossorigin is required for fonts
    even same-origin, or the preload is a second, wasted request (trap 29)."""
    assert TEMPLATES, "no templates matched"
    for t in TEMPLATES:
        text = t.read_text()
        assert PRELOAD in text, f"{t.name} has no font preload"
        assert STYLESHEET_TAG in text, f"{t.name} has no stylesheet tag {STYLESHEET_TAG!r}"
        assert text.index(PRELOAD) < text.index(STYLESHEET_TAG), f"{t.name}: preload must precede the stylesheet"
    missing = [p for p in built_pages() if PRELOAD not in p.read_text()]
    assert not missing, f"built pages without the preload (rebuild): {[str(p.relative_to(REPO)) for p in missing[:5]]}"


def test_display_rules_forbid_synthesised_styles():
    """(r) Recommendation, not a precedent: `font-synthesis: none` on the display rules stops the
    browser faux-bolding or faux-slanting a weight or style the file does not carry (trap 28). Bract
    does not use it. The font_synthesis slot records the decision: "adopted" runs the guard, "declined"
    skips it with the reason printed, anything else fails here naming both words (the first template
    shipped this as a pytest marker that failed as shipped and warned PytestUnknownMarkWarning; second
    dry run, finding 9). The declaration may sit in any rule that names the selector, a shared
    `h1, h2, .plate-title { font-synthesis: none }` included."""
    if FONT_SYNTHESIS == "declined":
        pytest.skip("font_synthesis is declined: the operator declined font-synthesis: none (decision recorded in the slot)")
    assert FONT_SYNTHESIS == "adopted", f"font_synthesis slot is {FONT_SYNTHESIS!r}; write adopted or declined"
    for selector in DISPLAY_SELECTORS:
        values = declared(selector, "font-synthesis")
        assert values and all(v == "none" for v in values), f"{selector} does not set font-synthesis: none (has {values})"


# --- t-u: optional guards for what happens before the font arrives and where a headline breaks ------------------------
def percent(value, what):
    m = re.fullmatch(r"([\d.]+)%", value.strip())
    assert m, f"{what}: expected a percentage, has {value!r}"
    return float(m.group(1))


def test_the_fallback_face_is_metric_matched_to_the_font(font):
    """(t) With font-display: swap the first paint is set in the fallback and then re-laid out in the font; a
    fallback of other metrics makes every headline jump (layout shift). A `local()` @font-face of the fallback
    with size-adjust and ascent-, descent- and line-gap-override removes most of it. This holds the DECLARATION to
    the shipped font: the face exists, names no url(), carries all four properties, its overrides agree with
    the font's hhea metrics (override x size-adjust = the font's own ascent, descent and line gap as a share of the
    em, within 0.5 points: a font rebuilt with other metrics fails here until the overrides are recomputed with
    scripts/fallback-metrics.py), and every display stack lists the family and then this fallback. NOT held: that
    size-adjust matches the fallback's real widths, which needs the fallback's file (the script computes it).
    Skips with its reason when the fallback_family slot is empty."""
    if not FALLBACK_FAMILY:
        pytest.skip("fallback_family is empty: the operator declined (or has not yet written) a fallback face with metric overrides")
    faces = [declarations(body) for head, body in css_blocks() if head == "@font-face" and first_family(declarations(body).get("font-family", "")) == FALLBACK_FAMILY]
    assert len(faces) == 1, f"{len(faces)} @font-face rules for {FALLBACK_FAMILY}; exactly one is expected"
    f = faces[0]
    src = f.get("src", "")
    assert "local(" in src and not URL_IN_CSS.findall(src), f"the fallback face must be a local() face with no url(), has src {src!r}"
    for prop in ("size-adjust", "ascent-override", "descent-override", "line-gap-override"):
        assert prop in f, f"the fallback face lacks {prop}"
    size = percent(f["size-adjust"], "size-adjust")
    em = font["head"].unitsPerEm
    for prop, own in (("ascent-override", font["hhea"].ascent), ("descent-override", -font["hhea"].descent), ("line-gap-override", font["hhea"].lineGap)):
        got = percent(f[prop], prop) * size / 100
        assert abs(got - 100 * own / em) <= 0.5, \
            f"{prop} {f[prop]} x size-adjust {f['size-adjust']} = {got:.1f}% of the em, but the font's {prop.split('-')[0]} is {100 * own / em:.1f}%; recompute the values with scripts/fallback-metrics.py"
    for selector in DISPLAY_SELECTORS:
        stacks = [[x.strip().strip("'\"") for x in v.split(",")] for v in declared(selector, "font-family")]
        assert stacks, f"{selector} sets no font-family"
        assert all(st[:2] == [FAMILY, FALLBACK_FAMILY] for st in stacks), f"{selector}: the stack must start {FAMILY}, {FALLBACK_FAMILY} (has {stacks})"


def test_display_headings_balance_their_lines():
    """(u) `text-wrap: balance` spreads a wrapped headline over equal lines, so a last line of one short word
    (a widow) needs no hand-placed no-break space in the content. Every `text-wrap` value declared for each
    listed selector must be balance. LIMIT, stated so nobody reads more into a pass: declarations on the listed selectors
    only; the cascade is not computed (no browser path here), so `.hero { text-wrap: wrap }` overriding `h1` on
